WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:02.701
-Take the love in.
We love you, Daniel Craig.

00:00:02.701 --> 00:00:03.936
Come on.
-That's incredible.

00:00:03.936 --> 00:00:06.138
Thank you. Incredible.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:06.138 --> 00:00:07.807
Thank you.

00:00:07.807 --> 00:00:09.508
-I want to get into all of this.

00:00:09.508 --> 00:00:12.578
And I want to thank you
for coming back to our show.

00:00:12.578 --> 00:00:16.115
I think you're the only guest
in the history of our show

00:00:16.115 --> 00:00:19.018
to come on our program
three times for the same movie.

00:00:19.018 --> 00:00:20.253
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:00:20.253 --> 00:00:21.454
The third time's
the charm, right?

00:00:21.454 --> 00:00:24.390
-Exactly. Yeah.
-It's gonna work this time.

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:26.292
-It's finally out.
-Yeah.

00:00:26.292 --> 00:00:28.227
-I loved it so much.
Everyone is loving it.

00:00:28.227 --> 00:00:29.895
It's number-1 movie
over the weekend.

00:00:29.895 --> 00:00:30.896
Congratulations.
-Thank you.

00:00:30.896 --> 00:00:32.798
-"No Time to Die."
-Thank you.

00:00:32.798 --> 00:00:35.267
Yeah.
-It's worth the wait.

00:00:35.267 --> 00:00:37.036
It's worth the wait.

00:00:37.036 --> 00:00:41.006
I also saw this
"Being James Bond" documentary

00:00:41.006 --> 00:00:42.074
about you on Apple TV.

00:00:42.074 --> 00:00:43.443
-Mm-hmm.

00:00:43.443 --> 00:00:45.611
-And I just didn't realize
how much work goes into it.

00:00:45.611 --> 00:00:47.313
You think like,
"Oh, if you get James Bond,"

00:00:47.313 --> 00:00:48.414
you go, "That's it.

00:00:48.414 --> 00:00:50.550
That's the best news
in the world."

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:51.818
-It is, by the way.
-Yes.

00:00:51.818 --> 00:00:53.619
-Yeah, yeah.
-That's true. It is, yeah.

00:00:53.619 --> 00:00:55.121
But it's also very hard,

00:00:55.121 --> 00:00:56.856
because you got
slammed immediately.

00:00:56.856 --> 00:00:58.858
They're like,
"A blond James Bond?"

00:00:58.858 --> 00:01:00.459
-Yeah.
-"Who is this guy?"

00:01:00.459 --> 00:01:02.561
-I mean, Barbara Broccoli
always points out that

00:01:02.561 --> 00:01:03.696
Roger Moore was blond,

00:01:03.696 --> 00:01:06.232
so I don't know why they
latched on to that.

00:01:06.232 --> 00:01:07.767
-Because it rhymed --
"Blond Bond" -- I guess.

00:01:07.767 --> 00:01:09.068
-You're right.
I suppose you're right, yeah.

00:01:09.068 --> 00:01:11.070
I mean, but I --
I don't know.

00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:13.039
People are very passionate
about Bond.

00:01:13.039 --> 00:01:14.740
I think they're
still passionate about Bond,

00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:16.175
and they certainly were
when I took over.

00:01:16.175 --> 00:01:17.943
So, you know, I kind of --

00:01:17.943 --> 00:01:21.514
You can't blame people
for getting a bit wound up.

00:01:21.514 --> 00:01:24.016
-I know, but then you have to
read these kind of

00:01:24.016 --> 00:01:25.351
reviews about yourself before
you even shoot a scene...

00:01:25.351 --> 00:01:26.452
-Yeah.

00:01:26.452 --> 00:01:27.553
-...saying like,
"Oh, I don't know if

00:01:27.553 --> 00:01:28.688
he's good-looking enough.

00:01:28.688 --> 00:01:29.722
I don't know if
he's tough enough."

00:01:29.722 --> 00:01:31.391
-But that's true,
so that's fine.

00:01:31.391 --> 00:01:32.958
-No, please.

00:01:32.958 --> 00:01:34.593
No, I just -- But I love that,
in the documentary,

00:01:34.593 --> 00:01:35.795
you see this and you go,

00:01:35.795 --> 00:01:37.796
"Oh, all this stuff
is going in your head."

00:01:37.796 --> 00:01:39.399
You're reading these reviews
and you go,

00:01:39.399 --> 00:01:40.400
"I'm not going to
read it anymore.

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:41.467
I'm just gonna go work."
-Yeah.

00:01:41.467 --> 00:01:42.902
-And then you go to the set.
-Yeah.

00:01:42.902 --> 00:01:44.704
-And everyone on the set
are reading those same articles.

00:01:44.704 --> 00:01:46.505
-Are looking at
their feet and wondering,

00:01:46.505 --> 00:01:47.507
"How's this gonna go?"

00:01:47.507 --> 00:01:48.775
-Yeah.
-But what do you do?

00:01:48.775 --> 00:01:49.909
You put your head down.

00:01:49.909 --> 00:01:51.644
I knew we had an amazing script.

00:01:51.644 --> 00:01:54.246
I knew we had a great director,
we had an amazing cast.

00:01:54.246 --> 00:01:57.116
We were doing stunts which,
at the time,

00:01:57.116 --> 00:01:58.885
I knew were incredible.

00:01:58.885 --> 00:02:00.253
I didn't know how good
they were gonna be,

00:02:00.253 --> 00:02:02.655
but I knew that they
were gonna be pretty great.

00:02:02.655 --> 00:02:05.992
And you think,
"Well, that's all we can do."

00:02:05.992 --> 00:02:07.193
You do your best.

00:02:07.193 --> 00:02:08.728
And then, if you don't like it,
fair enough.

00:02:08.728 --> 00:02:10.062
I'll walk away and say,
"I gave it a go."

00:02:10.062 --> 00:02:11.498
-But they found you.

00:02:11.498 --> 00:02:13.833
You were doing these kind of
independent films, art films.

00:02:13.833 --> 00:02:15.201
-Yeah.

00:02:15.201 --> 00:02:17.237
-And they go -- And then you
trained seven days a week.

00:02:17.237 --> 00:02:18.338
-Yeah.

00:02:18.338 --> 00:02:19.405
-And all you needed
was that one --

00:02:19.405 --> 00:02:20.473
-Eight days of week.

00:02:20.473 --> 00:02:21.708
-Sorry. Eight days a week.
Of course.

00:02:21.708 --> 00:02:23.442
You came --
You did that one scene.

00:02:23.442 --> 00:02:26.279
You came out of the water
in the ocean, in the swimsuit,

00:02:26.279 --> 00:02:28.280
and paparazzi got you,
and that's it.

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:30.616
That's our Bond right there.
That's the stud.

00:02:30.616 --> 00:02:34.253
That is Daniel Craig forever.
He is a stud.

00:02:34.253 --> 00:02:35.321
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:35.321 --> 00:02:37.323
This is your fifth film.

00:02:37.323 --> 00:02:39.892
You've done this for 15 years.
-Mm-hmm.

00:02:39.892 --> 00:02:42.862
-So far, the movies
have made over $3.2 billion.

00:02:42.862 --> 00:02:45.064
-That's kind of --
I didn't know that.

00:02:45.064 --> 00:02:46.498
-That's insane, right?
-That's incredible.

00:02:46.498 --> 00:02:47.867
Yeah, yeah.
-Yeah.

00:02:47.867 --> 00:02:51.037
-And I want to say that
you left your mark, obviously,

00:02:51.037 --> 00:02:53.172
and now we can call you
Commander Craig,

00:02:53.172 --> 00:02:54.541
because the British Royal Navy

00:02:54.541 --> 00:02:55.874
just made you
an honorary commander.

00:02:55.874 --> 00:02:58.811
-They did. I know. Yes. Yes.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:58.811 --> 00:03:00.713
I know.
-That's amazing, right?

00:03:00.713 --> 00:03:03.749
-I mean, my father --
I mean, it's really nice for me.

00:03:03.749 --> 00:03:06.218
My father was in the Navy.

00:03:06.218 --> 00:03:08.955
He sadly passed last year.
And it meant a lot.

00:03:08.955 --> 00:03:13.492
And what an honor,
an incredible thing.

00:03:13.492 --> 00:03:17.096
I met the First Sea Lord,
who has now just become

00:03:17.096 --> 00:03:19.598
commander of all
British Armed Forces.

00:03:19.598 --> 00:03:21.567
He's just been promoted.

00:03:21.567 --> 00:03:25.038
And we did a very small ceremony
because of COVID.

00:03:25.038 --> 00:03:28.207
I mean, the idea is,
I go onto a naval ship

00:03:28.207 --> 00:03:29.308
and I get piped aboard.

00:03:29.308 --> 00:03:31.777
I get a suit made,
a Savile Row tailor.

00:03:31.777 --> 00:03:33.111
I mean, whole bit, the hat.
-Ooh!

00:03:33.111 --> 00:03:35.348
-Yeah. And I get --
But we'll do that soon, I hope.

00:03:35.348 --> 00:03:36.682
-Yeah.

00:03:36.682 --> 00:03:38.984
-And I can put it on my
passport, I think, which --

00:03:38.984 --> 00:03:40.753
-Can you really? Hey, wow!
-Yeah, I know.

00:03:40.753 --> 00:03:42.488
-Yeah, I think people
know who you are.

00:03:42.488 --> 00:03:43.656
You don't need a
passport anymore, yeah.

00:03:43.656 --> 00:03:45.091
-Okay, maybe.

00:03:45.091 --> 00:03:48.194
-But I do also want to say,
after seeing the documentary,

00:03:48.194 --> 00:03:49.361
how great the producers,

00:03:49.361 --> 00:03:54.333
Barbara Broccoli
and Michael G. Wilson, did.

00:03:54.333 --> 00:03:57.437
Amazing attention to detail
and just keeping --

00:03:57.437 --> 00:03:59.304
going, "No, this is our guy.

00:03:59.304 --> 00:04:00.740
We're sticking with him."

00:04:00.740 --> 00:04:02.775
And then you all came together,
and it's just the passion,

00:04:02.775 --> 00:04:04.910
the love that goes into
every scene.

00:04:04.910 --> 00:04:07.413
-Well, they make --
You know, they do this thing.

00:04:07.413 --> 00:04:09.949
They make a family,
and, like any family,

00:04:09.949 --> 00:04:11.217
there's ups and downs,
you know,

00:04:11.217 --> 00:04:13.619
a little bit of back-and-forth,
which is great.

00:04:13.619 --> 00:04:15.755
But, ultimately,
we've got each other's backs

00:04:15.755 --> 00:04:17.389
and we're looking out
for each other.

00:04:17.389 --> 00:04:19.992
And that is really the --

00:04:19.992 --> 00:04:21.961
You know, that's
what makes them so special.

00:04:21.961 --> 00:04:23.829
I mean, every movie is
a creative process,

00:04:23.829 --> 00:04:26.232
and you kind of have great --
You know, I love my job,

00:04:26.232 --> 00:04:28.401
so every day is great for me.

00:04:28.401 --> 00:04:29.535
But when you're on a Bond movie,

00:04:29.535 --> 00:04:31.904
you kind of realize
this is -- it's special.

00:04:31.904 --> 00:04:33.138
There's nothing quite like it.

00:04:33.138 --> 00:04:35.675
And then you've got all
that talent around you

00:04:35.675 --> 00:04:39.712
and this passionate --
And particularly on this movie,

00:04:39.712 --> 00:04:41.647
there was just --
From every department,

00:04:41.647 --> 00:04:43.382
they were just sort of
willing it

00:04:43.382 --> 00:04:45.284
to kind of be
the best we could make it.

00:04:45.284 --> 00:04:48.320
-Was it super-emotional because
you knew it was the last one?

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:51.557
-I just -- I mean, it wasn't
while we were filming it.

00:04:51.557 --> 00:04:53.492
I don't have time to think about
that sort of thing.

00:04:53.492 --> 00:04:54.860
You always think
about tomorrow or next week

00:04:54.860 --> 00:04:56.261
or whatever you're
kind of doing.

00:04:56.261 --> 00:04:58.064
There's always sort of fires
to put out and things to do.

00:04:58.064 --> 00:05:03.269
But the last night,
it was like 2:00 in the morning.

00:05:03.269 --> 00:05:06.205
It's back lot at Pinewood.
It's pissing with rain.

00:05:06.205 --> 00:05:10.809
It's, like -- It's kind of
apt as it could be.

00:05:10.809 --> 00:05:13.446
And all I do is, I'm in a tuxedo
and I walk down --

00:05:13.446 --> 00:05:14.580
It's in the movie.

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:16.282
I walk down an alleyway,
and that's it.

00:05:16.282 --> 00:05:17.383
It's my last scene.

00:05:17.383 --> 00:05:19.718
And most movies,
when you finish them,

00:05:19.718 --> 00:05:21.154
they're just big anticlimaxes.

00:05:21.154 --> 00:05:22.455
You sort of --
Everybody's so tired.

00:05:22.455 --> 00:05:23.989
You're just like,
"Yeah, see you, bye."

00:05:23.989 --> 00:05:25.624
You know, want to get home.
-Yeah.

00:05:25.624 --> 00:05:30.730
-And every single person
in the office had stayed late.

00:05:30.730 --> 00:05:33.633
All the cast had stayed late.
All the crew had stayed late.

00:05:33.633 --> 00:05:34.967
And all the heads of department.
They all came.

00:05:34.967 --> 00:05:37.002
Suddenly, there's 200 people
standing there,

00:05:37.002 --> 00:05:39.505
and I had to make a speech,
which is just the last thing...

00:05:39.505 --> 00:05:41.640
[ Laughter ]

00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:42.942
-I could never do it.

00:05:42.942 --> 00:05:45.544
I mean, but, also, these faces
that you've seen change.

00:05:45.544 --> 00:05:47.212
For 15 years,
you've seen these faces

00:05:47.212 --> 00:05:48.247
and you're saying goodbye.

00:05:48.247 --> 00:05:49.582
-We all got
older together, yeah.

00:05:49.582 --> 00:05:50.850
-Yeah, it's kind of amazing.

00:05:50.850 --> 00:05:52.885
But is there any way --

00:05:52.885 --> 00:05:55.855
Should I set up the film
at all for this

00:05:55.855 --> 00:05:57.189
or just say it's another --

00:05:57.189 --> 00:05:59.392
-What? Are we gonna look
at something?

00:05:59.392 --> 00:06:00.626
-Yeah, we'll look at a clip.

00:06:00.626 --> 00:06:01.860
-I don't think you
need to set it up.

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:04.229
The DB5's in it.
-Yeah, everyone knows it.

00:06:04.229 --> 00:06:05.698
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:06:05.698 --> 00:06:07.667
I want to show --
Here's Daniel Craig

00:06:07.667 --> 00:06:09.601
as James Bond in
"No Time to Die."

00:06:09.601 --> 00:06:10.803
Take a look at this.

00:06:12.572 --> 00:06:15.408
[ Whirring ]

00:06:17.343 --> 00:06:19.479
[ Engine revs, tires squeal ]

00:06:26.986 --> 00:06:29.589
[ Engine revving ]

00:06:39.064 --> 00:06:40.600
-[ Breathing deeply ]

00:06:47.506 --> 00:06:48.941
[ Screaming ]

00:06:52.278 --> 00:06:54.313
[ Tires screech ]

00:07:00.920 --> 00:07:02.588
[ Tires screech ]

00:07:02.588 --> 00:07:05.124
-Come on, now!
That's what you want!

00:07:05.124 --> 00:07:06.359
That's a movie right there!

